  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nA ridge remains in place over the southeastern US Friday and into \nthe beginning of the weekend. This ridge has kept things mostly dry \nbut precip chances begin to increase Fri afternoon as the ridge \nbegins to weaken. The models are showing a significant band of \nmoisture associated with a weak frontal boundary moving northward \nout of the Gulf and northern FL Fri. With the ridge in place across \nnorth GA this boundary stalls across south GA but does increases \nprecip chances across central GA due to its proximity. With the \nridge weakening as we go further into the weekend a backdoor cold \nfront moves SW out of the Carolinas and into NE GA Sun afternoon. \nThis front is fairly weak and looses a lot of its strength as it \npushes into the old weakening ridge. Slightly cooler air is expected \nbehind the frontal boundary on Monday, as a ridge of high pressure \ncentered over Hudson Bay sinks southward. There still remains the \npotential for diurnal convection, Mon/Tue with isolated to scattered \nafternoon/evening thunderstorms. \n\nLittle change in the temperature trends are likely through the \nweekend but will see slightly cooler temps Mon/Tue after the frontal \nboundary passes through. Higher than average temperatures will \nremain in place - with temperatures around 8-13 degrees higher than \nclimatology through Sunday. Daytime highs will be in the upper 80s \nto upper 90s, with heat indices from around 90 to 106F.",
